# Reports are generated. Almost all of them are scratch and stay out of the
# repo.
*
!.gitignore

# EXCEPT the dev-set evaluation result, which IS committed. The README quotes
# these numbers, and a quoted number with no artifact behind it is a claim
# nobody can check. tests/test_m5_eval.py compares a live run against this
# file, so a regression shows up as a diff rather than as a README that
# quietly stopped being true.
#
# It is 3KB and deterministic: same seed, same config, same bytes.
#
# reports/eval-holdout/ IS committed, and this comment previously said it would
# not be. Changed deliberately: every other quoted number in this project has an
# artifact behind it (results.json, bench.md, ai_loop.json), and a holdout figure
# is the one number that most needs to be checkable rather than trusted. It is
# written once and never regenerated.
!eval/
eval/*
!eval/results.json
!eval/results.md
# throughput.md is committed on the bench.md principle, not the results.json
# one: durations differ run to run, so this is evidence of a real run on a
# stated machine rather than a byte-stable artifact. It exists because the
# holdout was run BEFORE the runner was instrumented and so has no throughput
# figure at all - committing the dev one is what makes that absence checkable
# instead of a claim. tests/test_timing.py 22 asserts the holdout still has no
# counterpart to this file.
!eval/throughput.md
!eval-holdout/
eval-holdout/*
!eval-holdout/results.json
!eval-holdout/results.md

# bench.md is committed for the same reason: the README quotes its throughput
# figures, and the machine spec in its header is what makes them a measurement
# rather than a boast. Durations differ run to run - that is what a median of
# three is for - so this file is evidence of a real run on a stated machine,
# not a byte-stable artifact like results.json.
!bench.md

# The M7 loop result is committed for the same reason: the README quotes the
# 27/507 ceiling and the adversarial zero, and a quoted number with no artifact
# behind it is unfalsifiable. Deterministic - no model is involved.
!ai/
ai/*
!ai/ai_loop.json
# The store-path measurement is committed for the same reason, and for one
# more: it is the SECOND of two AI numbers, and a reader has to be able to
# check that it is a different measurement rather than a revision of the
# first. It also carries the pairing key, which is a published rule.
!ai/store_path.json
# THE REAL-MODEL SAMPLE, and it was missed when its two siblings above were
# un-ignored. Same rule, same reason, and the omission was not visible from
# inside a working tree that already had the file: three tests in test_ai.py
# read it, so they passed here and failed in every fresh clone - two of them as
# a raw FileNotFoundError. It also carries the ONLY copy of the 24/40 and 33/40
# the README publishes, which were unfalsifiable for exactly as long.
#
# It is the one artifact here that cost money to produce. That is an argument
# for committing it, not against: `make record-fixtures` is the only target
# that spends, and nobody should have to spend again to check a published
# number.
!ai/real_model_sample.json

# The rendered queue is committed - the README links it and the screenshots come
# from it. state.db is NOT: it is a build output of `make demo-state`, it is
# large, and a committed database would drift from the code that writes it.
!ui/
ui/*
!ui/queue.html
!ui/evidence-queue.png
!ui/streamlit-queue.png
!ui/evidence-panel.png
# The OUTAGE page and its screenshots. Committed because the two waiting
# states being visibly different is a claim about what a reviewer SEES, and
# an assertion that two hex strings differ is not the same evidence. The
# store behind them (outage.db) stays out, like state.db.
!ui/queue-outage.html
!ui/queue-outage.png
!ui/streamlit-outage.png
# The AI_VERIFIED rows, which sit at ranks 58-59 and are the reason the page
# stopped truncating at 40.
!ui/queue-ai-verified.png

# THE M9 EXPORT, committed on the same rule as everything above it: the README
# publishes the balanced-journal total and the voucher count, and until now
# reports/export/ held ZERO committed files - so Rs2,781,220.13 was a number no
# artifact contained. The XML carries the 32 ledger amounts that sum to it;
# summary.json states it outright, because a figure a reader has to derive by
# addition is not one they can check at a glance.
#
# Both are deterministic: same store, same results.json, same bytes, and the
# idempotency key in the filename is a function of the content.
!export/
export/*
!export/summary.json
!export/tally-2026-11-30-c6ded63298100e2c.xml
